WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.431 --> 00:00:13.419
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.386 --> 00:00:18.260
DAVID ARONCHICK: "Cloud
OnAir," live webinars

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.520
from Google Cloud.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.980
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.210
My name is David Aronchick.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:26.480
And today, I will be
talking about Kubeflow.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.710
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform,

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.050
and we have Googlers
standing by to answer them.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.070
Let's get started.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:37.690
So as I mentioned I will be
talking about Kubeflow today.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.730
Kubeflow is a brand new machine
learning platform designed

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:46.230
to make using machine learning
very, very easy on Kubernetes.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.970
And the reason we
really approached

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.790
this problem is because we
heard over and over again

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.100
that customers are
looking to take advantage

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.680
of the great Kubernetes
platform and all

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.370
it provides, relative to
cloud-native applications,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.770
and bring those same
kind of innovations

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.870
to machine learning.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.720
Before I begin getting
into Kubeflow specifically,

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.390
a lot of people ask what's so
great about machine learning?

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.070
You hear about it all the time.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:13.200
It's in the news.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.804
You hear about all the great
new things that are coming out,

00:01:15.804 --> 00:01:17.970
and people want to understand,
does machine learning

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.020
affect me?

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.570
Can I use machine learning
to change my business?

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:24.450
And the reality is you
really, really can.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.470
What you see here is a
graph of Google data centers

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:29.940
and the amount of
energy that they

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.360
use per the amount of
compute that they create.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.580
These are data
center engineers, who

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.880
spend their entire life getting
fantastic at tuning data

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.000
centers, making sure they run
properly, making sure they

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.860
are as efficient as possible.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.840
They use all the
latest technologies.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.020
They use 100% renewable
energy, and they really, really

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.570
spend all their
time focusing on how

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:55.710
to make our compute to provide
queries and Google Cloud

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.335
as efficient as possible.

00:01:57.335 --> 00:01:58.710
When the team came
together, they

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.950
said what could we do next,
they looked at machine learning.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.730
And as you can see here, simply
by applying machine learning,

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:10.860
they were able to save 40% over
already the best-tuned data

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.420
centers in the world.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.240
And that's exactly what
machine learning does.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.240
When they first saw these
results, they shook their head.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.350
They couldn't believe
what was going on.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.110
But this is the largest
expense they had,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.660
and they were able to do
that through standard machine

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.340
learning techniques.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.260
However, machine
learning is very hard.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:34.200
People understand the standards
of programming and computing

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.910
and how to rollout
systems, and they've

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:39.040
been building those experiences
over the past 40 years.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.040
But unfortunately,
the reality is

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.000
that there's a big separation
between all that ML goodness

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.885
that you heard on one side
and where people are today.

00:02:47.885 --> 00:02:49.260
They don't know
whether or not it

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.470
applies to what their current
business problems are.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:55.410
They don't know how to make
those applications work.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.390
And they don't know,
even once you roll out

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:59.040
those applications,
if they can really

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.105
diagnose what's going on under
the hood, because a lot of ML

00:03:02.105 --> 00:03:04.920
can be really inscrutable.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.790
Why is it so complicated?

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:09.840
It's basically because it comes
down to these two situations.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.160
Today, there really aren't any
great prepackaged ML solutions,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.140
and there's a whole bunch
of reasons for this.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.090
But basically, it comes down
to one of the two following

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:18.870
things--

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.870
either one, you have to
set up your entire system

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:22.860
from scratch.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.380
That means integrating
with legacy systems.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.450
That means customizing,
picking, and choosing

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:28.620
different solutions.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.470
And eventually, once
you spend all that time

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:35.010
getting your system in exactly
the right size and shape,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.410
you then have to migrate it
because your business needs

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:38.160
change.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.830
Maybe you need to move
to new geo or locality,

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.720
maybe you have data in
different locations,

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.428
and you want to run
your machine learning

00:03:44.428 --> 00:03:45.450
in different locations.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:49.110
All those problems
now surface, and you

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.090
have to re-setup
all your systems

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:54.360
and re-integrate with brand
new set of legacy systems.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.960
Or the alternative is you use
an entirely hosted solution.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.600
And in the first five
minutes, that's great.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.330
You get a first
look at exactly what

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:04.800
machine learning can provide.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.110
Unfortunately, the
next five years

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.050
are nothing but pain
because you have everything

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.320
that you had on the left.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.180
You have the same
issues setting it up.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.390
You have the same issues
integrating with your legacy

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:16.230
systems.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.089
And when it comes
to migrating, you're

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:20.010
now out of options
because you're locked

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.240
into a single deployment.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.310
And certainly, not all
hosted solutions fall

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:25.650
into this category but
certainly, many of them

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:29.280
do because, in the
purpose of creating

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.470
those hosted solutions,
they've abstracted away

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.050
a lot of the customizability
and configurability

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:36.380
necessary for really
productionizing your ML

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:37.350
worklist.

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:41.140
So the question is, haven't
we heard this story before?

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:43.110
And the reality is we have.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:47.190
Before I came to
work on Kubeflow,

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:48.360
I worked on Kubernetes.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.440
And in 2015, when we first
got Kubernetes up and running,

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.490
we heard this over and
over and over again,

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.250
relative to containers
and Kubernetes.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.560
People were building their
own bespoke orchestrators

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:02.010
their own bespoke ways of
rolling out containers,

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:03.990
especially in
production-ready ways,

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.210
and integrating them with
their legacy solutions

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:08.110
and figuring out how
to get across clouds.

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.080
When Kubernetes came
along, it really

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.450
enabled us to build
cloud-native applications.

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:15.360
And when we say
cloud-native applications,

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.590
we say not just giving you
all the components in the box

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:22.470
in order to do service
discovery and manageability

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:24.990
and declarative
application rollout

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.000
but really, extensibility.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.730
About six months ago,
at KubeCon Austin,

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.490
we had a talk specifically
about the extensibility

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:33.780
of Kubernetes.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:37.340
What this meant is you
get very clean layers

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:40.720
in between all the ways that
you roll out your application,

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:43.740
meaning that if you want to
extend in a specific way,

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:47.010
you're able to use the native
APIs without forking the code,

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:50.370
without having some bespoke
hardwired, welded together

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:50.982
solution.

00:05:50.982 --> 00:05:52.440
You're able to use
those components

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.990
and extend it in the
way that make the most

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:57.660
sense for your business.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.670
And what this
ultimately resulted in

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:01.680
was cloud-native apps.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:05.520
These are applications designed
to run in a distributed way.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:07.480
They're loosely
coupled together,

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:10.680
meaning you can swap in and out,
yet they're extremely scalable.

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.070
They're designed to handle
the realities of a modern data

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:15.960
center and modern application.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:17.130
The single node goes down.

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:18.240
It knows how to recover.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.230
If you need to migrate from A
to B, it knows how to do that.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:22.980
If you need to use
core components,

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.510
like service discovery
or mounting storage,

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:26.850
you can handle that as well.

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:30.060
And it allows you and
your software engineers

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.820
to focus on just the components
that are the most important

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:35.100
for you, the things
that actually make

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:36.790
a difference for your business.

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:41.220
So what we would propose is that
we need the same capabilities

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:45.420
relative to cloud-native
ML, the same loose coupling,

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:48.510
the same scalability,
the same portability.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:52.000
And for us, like I said, it does
come down to those components.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:53.970
We want it to be
highly composable.

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:55.734
We want it to be
highly portable,

00:06:55.734 --> 00:06:57.275
and we want it to
be highly scalable.

00:06:57.275 --> 00:07:01.600
By composability, we mean
everyone focuses, when it comes

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:03.760
to ML, on building a model.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:06.300
You might hear this
over and over again.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:12.460
A brand new paper came out
about detecting diabetes just

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:16.990
through retinal scans, improving
the way that energy deployment

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:19.720
is used and all the
advances you get there,

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:23.620
discovering a new protein
through exploring space.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:26.420
Those are fantastic
models, but the reality

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:28.960
is that when you go and
talk to any data scientist,

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.750
they see that the problem
is much, much larger.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:33.940
It goes beyond just
building a model

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:37.330
to the actual end-to-end
stack of building

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:38.860
an overall deployment.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:41.620
That includes data ingestion,
transforming the data,

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:45.100
exploring it, splitting it,
finally building your model,

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:47.499
and then validating that
the model is accurate.

00:07:47.499 --> 00:07:49.540
And then rolling it out
to production-- something

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:52.060
that very frequently
is overlooked.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:53.590
How do you roll it
out and track it?

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:55.750
How do you make sure it
doesn't drift over time?

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:58.291
How do you make sure it's better
than what you have out there

00:07:58.291 --> 00:07:59.176
today?

00:07:59.176 --> 00:08:00.800
That is what we were
talking about when

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:02.120
we say composability.

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:05.960
We need to provide all those
components to really allow you

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:07.850
for a production-ready
environment,

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:10.460
and especially because
every enterprise is

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:11.930
going to be slightly different.

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:13.649
One team might use Spark.

00:08:13.649 --> 00:08:14.690
Another might use Hadoop.

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:17.960
Another might use their
own custom data processing.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:19.070
You might use TensorFlow.

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:22.310
You might use PyTorch,
Caffe, MXNet--

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:24.830
doesn't matter-- or rolling
out the production--

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:26.540
whether or not you're
using Kubernetes

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:30.470
or you're on potentially
a hosted model serving.

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:32.282
All those components
are complicated,

00:08:32.282 --> 00:08:33.740
and we want to give
you the ability

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:35.360
to natively choose
the things that

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:37.320
make the most sense for you.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:40.100
Secondly, it comes
to portability.

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.929
When we think about
portability, this

00:08:41.929 --> 00:08:44.030
is the reality of the world.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:46.100
That entire stack, that
I described earlier,

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:48.897
that is just a component
of all the configuration

00:08:48.897 --> 00:08:49.730
that you might have.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:53.330
You run times, drivers,
OSes, accelerators all

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:55.640
under the hood, let alone
all those compositions

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:58.370
that you have above.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:02.960
However, once you spend all that
time getting your experiment up

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:05.417
and running and so
on, then you have

00:09:05.417 --> 00:09:08.000
to roll it out into a variety
of different environments, which

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:09.665
might be very, very different.

00:09:09.665 --> 00:09:11.540
You could have something
for experimentation,

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:14.300
something completely different
for on-prem or cloud-based

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:17.000
training, and then finally,
potentially rolling out

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.840
to the cloud
public-private on-prem

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:22.970
for actually serving
it and integrating

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:24.742
with your application.

00:09:24.742 --> 00:09:26.450
So that becomes more
and more challenging

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:28.880
because all that configuration
you did in one environment

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:31.460
now requires a
second set of effort

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:34.220
to go and replicate it for
these new environments.

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:36.746
And then finally, we
think about scalability.

00:09:36.746 --> 00:09:38.870
A lot of people, when they
think about scalability,

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:40.786
it might just be more
nodes, but it's actually

00:09:40.786 --> 00:09:42.815
far more complicated than that.

00:09:42.815 --> 00:09:44.315
You could think
about whether or not

00:09:44.315 --> 00:09:47.960
you're using CPUs, GPUs,
Google's brand new TensorFlow

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:51.230
processing units,
more CPUs per node--

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:54.950
are you using those properly--
disk networking concerns?

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:57.210
And then there's things
that are not just hardware.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.780
How do you scale
up for skill sets?

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:04.350
Potentially, an intern to a data
scientist, a software engineer,

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:06.620
researcher, any of
those various things--

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:09.110
those are scalability
components as well.

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:11.450
You talk about teams.

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:13.280
Are the teams geolocated?

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:15.320
Does the team grow,
shrink over time?

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:18.590
And then finally,
number of experiments--

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:21.890
the most sophisticated
machine learning shops

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:23.810
are in fact running
thousands of experiments

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:26.090
simultaneously, testing
them off each other

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:28.010
and seeing what's
working and then tracking

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:29.540
those experiments over time.

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:32.610
All those fit into the
components of scale.

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:34.820
So those are the
three key components

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:37.260
that we want to bring
to cloud-native ML.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:38.840
And as I said, the
things that are

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:40.460
great at that we already know.

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:42.320
It's containers in Kubernetes.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:47.150
Except if you want to use
machine learning on Kubernetes,

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:50.090
unfortunately, you have to
get really, really smart

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:51.770
at a whole bunch of things--

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:54.500
containers, packaging,
service endpoints,

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:56.460
scaling, on and on and on.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.900
These are all new concepts,
even for the most sophisticated

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:03.410
application teams, and
now layering Kubernetes

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:06.690
on top of that is a
lot for ML scientists

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:09.170
and data scientists to grasp.

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:11.930
So that is why we
introduced Kubeflow.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:15.230
The idea behind it is to
make it easy for everyone

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:19.640
to develop, deploy, and manage
portable, distributed machine

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:21.680
learning on Kubernetes.

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:23.970
And for us, it looks
like the following.

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:26.150
The first is you think
about Kubernetes.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:27.890
It provides a
wonderful abstraction

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:32.630
layer and a set of core services
and native APIs available.

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:37.550
And then you describe your
deployment for Kubeflow

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:39.830
using code, and you're
able to roll out

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:42.020
the exact same deployment
in all these places.

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:44.000
So that complicated
pipeline that

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.250
has been customized
exactly as you like

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:48.810
is now swappable for any
of these environments,

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:51.130
based on your needs.

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:53.380
And specifically, you
know two weeks ago,

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:56.260
at KubeCon in Europe, we
were pleased to announce

00:11:56.260 --> 00:12:02.260
Kubeflow 0.1, our first official
release of our new platform.

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:04.810
Specifically, in
the box today, we

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:08.320
have a Jupyter notebook and
specifically JupyterHub.

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:11.680
We offer the tools for
multiarchitecture distributed

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:15.160
training, meaning you're
now able to use many nodes

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:17.860
and many accelerators on those
nodes in a very straightforward

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:18.580
way.

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:20.530
We also offer
multiframework serving

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:24.220
so you're able to use server
things like extra boost

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:28.852
and TensorFlow and so on using
standard components in the box.

00:12:28.852 --> 00:12:31.060
We have a number of different
examples and workflows,

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.310
and best of all,
that last point,

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:36.550
we use ksonnet packaging to
allow you to customize it

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:38.354
for yourself.

00:12:38.354 --> 00:12:39.770
So with that, I'd
like to show you

00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:43.010
a brief demo of what
Kubeflow looks like to solve

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:46.080
a real-world business problem.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:48.860
One of the most common
things that people use today

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:50.910
is around sentiment analysis.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:56.060
So this might be where you
have a large body of text

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:59.090
that you want to
scan over and process

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:01.820
for understanding how
your business is reacting

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:03.260
to these various things.

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:05.316
I recorded this video
a little bit earlier.

00:13:05.316 --> 00:13:07.440
Unfortunately, training
takes a little bit of time.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:09.231
And we have a short
time, but I'll walk you

00:13:09.231 --> 00:13:11.340
through exactly what we do.

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.640
So here you see this
is the Yelp data set.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:15.860
It's published on Kaggle.

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:21.920
And it has many, many comments
about various restaurants

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:24.330
and things that people
might want to use.

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:27.950
And you can see here if we
looked at this middle thing,

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:32.180
it's a lot of text, and
standard text analysis might not

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:33.480
get at what you're looking for.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:36.470
In this case, a little on the
pricey end but the baked goods

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:37.370
were good.

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:39.260
And that's particularly
hard because you

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:41.780
use the same word meaning
two different things

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:42.837
in a single sentence.

00:13:42.837 --> 00:13:45.170
And so for that middle word,
we would probably say, wow,

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:46.420
that's actually a good review.

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:48.420
We should probably
see what happens.

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:50.960
So if I walk forward
and I look at how

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:54.740
it's wired into our
application, we'll go,

00:13:54.740 --> 00:13:58.140
and we'll click
on the sentiment.

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:01.330
And unfortunately, it
looks like we're way off,

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:03.310
and this is not at all uncommon.

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:05.880
You'll have something
where you have a coarse way

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:07.980
of approaching the problem.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:11.092
And even though a human can
figure out what's going on,

00:14:11.092 --> 00:14:12.300
it might be very complicated.

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:15.270
If we go and look at the
code, this is not so good.

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:21.240
We're using standard
techniques to look through

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:24.420
for synonyms of things that are
good and things that are bad.

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:26.592
Let's see if we can do better.

00:14:26.592 --> 00:14:28.050
Right now, what
I'm going to use is

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:29.790
something called Tensor2Tensor.

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:32.070
This is a standard machine
learning framework,

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:33.670
open sourced by Google.

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:36.510
It is designed to solve a
lot of these problems, where

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:39.480
you can basically take
large bodies of text,

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:41.340
paint them with
sentiment analysis,

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:44.970
say what's good and bad,
and roll them out to solve

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:46.920
some of these problems.

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:49.510
In this case, we've
already packaged it,

00:14:49.510 --> 00:14:54.540
and you can see here that it
supports sentiment analysis.

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:57.650
And what we'll do
is we'll roll it out

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.650
to Kubeflow specifically.

00:14:59.650 --> 00:15:02.910
So one of the most common
situations that we have today

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:06.030
is a data scientist might
not even have a Kubernetes

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:07.440
cluster available to them.

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:08.815
What they want to
do is they want

00:15:08.815 --> 00:15:11.130
to start on their local
laptop and figure out

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:17.280
how to explore a particular
model to see whether or not

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:18.750
it will solve their problem.

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:20.760
So right here, I am
executing the command.

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:23.160
This simply says
to my laptop I want

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:26.040
to focus on using Minikube.

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:29.010
Minikube is a free to
use local Kubernetes

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:31.680
deployment that lets you test
out a number of solutions,

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:34.420
before you roll them
out to production.

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:36.810
So with that, I'm
going to take this,

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:40.830
and I'm going to
apply Kubeflow core.

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:44.130
This is something where we
have the hosted registry,

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:48.090
and this is the core components
that exist as part of Kubeflow

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:49.680
and with that, that's it.

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:51.300
That's the single
command that I need

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:56.640
to execute in order to spin
up a Kubeflow deployment.

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:59.670
You can see here,
after that, it gives me

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:02.970
a JupyterHub, which is
the standard interface

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:05.430
for doing a number of
machine learning exploration.

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:07.950
I go, and I'm able to
select from a number

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:09.810
of different prebuilt solutions.

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:12.600
In this case, we have all these
various different versions

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:16.600
of TensorFlow, GPU,
CPU support, and I'm

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:19.230
able to straight
through this configure

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:23.380
it using various CPUs, memory,
and extra resource limits,

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:25.260
including things
like accelerators.

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:27.720
Again, we're trying to
make this very, very easy

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:32.740
to begin using TensorFlow.

00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:36.721
And with that, I now am going
to take that deployment--

00:16:36.721 --> 00:16:38.470
and like I said, I'm
not going to show you

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:39.640
the training because,
unfortunately, it

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:40.900
does take a little bit.

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:44.980
But locally, what I've done is
I've downloaded Tensor2Tensor,

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:46.760
and I've started running it.

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:49.630
And what you can see here
is that though I deployed

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:52.180
Kubeflow, which is very
straightforward, what

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:54.250
I want to do is I
want to configure it,

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:56.060
based on my local environment.

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:59.500
And in this case, I've been
able to say using parameters--

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:01.330
without changing
any of the code--

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:04.359
I can say, well, look, this
machine doesn't have a GPU,

00:17:04.359 --> 00:17:06.099
and I only need one worker.

00:17:06.099 --> 00:17:10.060
That's the way of running
these actual applications

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:11.180
and so on and so forth.

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:14.349
So using parameters,
not changing code

00:17:14.349 --> 00:17:16.150
or changing different
packages, I'm

00:17:16.150 --> 00:17:18.520
able to set various things here.

00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:21.220
And then I'll take
this, and I apply it.

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:23.290
And now I begin the
job of training.

00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:25.349
It's looking at
my local data set.

00:17:25.349 --> 00:17:26.740
It's looking at those nodes.

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:29.500
And you can see now with
just three commands,

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:32.710
I've gone from nothing
to a Kubeflow deployment

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:37.730
to now actually running a model
in a very straightforward way.

00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:41.710
And you can see here that the
model is running appropriately.

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:44.950
So with that, because
it's a laptop,

00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:46.630
that's not going to
train very quickly,

00:17:46.630 --> 00:17:48.820
but I can see that
there's no syntax errors.

00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:50.540
Everything is working properly.

00:17:50.540 --> 00:17:53.560
So I think it's appropriate that
we actually take this and now

00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:56.910
reapply it to the cloud.

00:17:56.910 --> 00:18:00.630
Before I began this demo, I
created a Kubernetes cluster

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:03.120
using Google Kubernetes engine.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:07.650
This is the overall
hosted service

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:11.670
that we use to host
Kubernetes compliant

00:18:11.670 --> 00:18:13.890
clusters on Google Cloud.

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:16.110
And in this case, I'm going
to use a native feature

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:22.300
inside of Kubernetes
engine called Node Pools.

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:26.730
Node Pools allow you to create
multiple sets of nodes that

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:29.110
exist within a single
Kubernetes cluster,

00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:32.310
and it lets me schedule
my jobs to the nodes that

00:18:32.310 --> 00:18:33.090
are appropriate.

00:18:33.090 --> 00:18:35.760
In this case, I'm going to
create one node pool that

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:41.560
has four nodes, each of which
have eight NVIDIA Tesla P100s.

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:43.590
And then I'm also going
to create a second node

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:47.290
pool that we are going to use
for scheduling our TPU jobs.

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:50.580
You can see there the eight
Tesla P100s associated

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:53.490
with those nodes,
and then we're also

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:55.745
going to create these TPUs.

00:18:55.745 --> 00:18:59.620
TPUs are Google's
Tensor Processing Units.

00:18:59.620 --> 00:19:01.890
Those are designed to run
TensorFlow particularly

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:03.980
quickly.

00:19:03.980 --> 00:19:07.790
And you can see them that
they are scaled up right now.

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:13.720
So with that, we're going
to go, and we're going

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:15.650
to switch our configuration.

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:18.130
So now, again, without
making any changes,

00:19:18.130 --> 00:19:20.770
I switched from looking
at my local cluster

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:22.640
to looking at the
one in the cloud.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:25.690
And now I'm going to apply
that exact same deployment

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:30.490
to the cloud, and it's going
to spin it up and run it.

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:35.530
From there, we are going to
roll out our job to, again,

00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:39.670
we're going to use
distributed TensorFlow.

00:19:39.670 --> 00:19:44.015
But before I do that, I'm going
to swap out those parameters.

00:19:44.015 --> 00:19:46.210
As I mentioned
earlier, before we said

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:47.650
we're not going to use any GPUs.

00:19:47.650 --> 00:19:50.770
Now we're going to
have one GPU per node.

00:19:50.770 --> 00:19:52.120
We're going to have two workers.

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:53.494
And we're going
to roll this out,

00:19:53.494 --> 00:19:57.100
and it's going to use a
different data directory,

00:19:57.100 --> 00:19:58.840
instead of using local.

00:19:58.840 --> 00:19:59.800
And there you go.

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:04.240
So now, again, you saw literally
everything we had to do.

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:07.090
We change some parameters,
and we changed our context.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:09.250
And we were able to roll
out the exact same thing

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:11.980
that we had locally to
this cluster in the cloud

00:20:11.980 --> 00:20:13.840
to have it run
much more quickly.

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:15.220
We made one more swap.

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:17.240
We had a prebuilt
cluster for TPUs,

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:19.480
and you do have to
build differently

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:21.910
based on GPUs or CPUs.

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:23.780
And we'll roll that out as well.

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:25.940
And so, again, we just
added one more command,

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:29.710
and now we're going to run
simultaneous experiments

00:20:29.710 --> 00:20:33.470
against both the TPUs and GPUs.

00:20:33.470 --> 00:20:37.240
As you can see here, we're
walking through the Google

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:38.650
Kubernetes Engine.

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:40.810
You can see the
logs running there,

00:20:40.810 --> 00:20:44.680
and you have the native support
for showing all the jobs that

00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:49.910
are running in your Google
Kubernetes Engine cluster.

00:20:49.910 --> 00:20:52.090
Now to see how
things are running,

00:20:52.090 --> 00:20:54.880
we're going to swap to
look at TensorBoard.

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:59.160
TensorBoard also comes as part
of the core set of components

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:01.520
in Kubeflow.

00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:04.570
And you can see here
that on our GPU cluster,

00:21:04.570 --> 00:21:09.340
we're doing about two to three
experiments per second, which

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:13.302
is perfectly fine
for a large data set,

00:21:13.302 --> 00:21:14.510
and then we're going to swap.

00:21:14.510 --> 00:21:18.260
And after looking at the
GP1, let's look at the TP1.

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:20.980
And on the TP1, we're actually
able to get it much faster.

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:23.240
We're actually doing
about 16 times a second.

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:25.210
So we're able to
run that experiment

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:30.010
and see that in this particular
case, we get about a five times

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:33.880
acceleration of moving
from GPUs to TPUs.

00:21:33.880 --> 00:21:35.380
Now that's not always the case.

00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:37.200
GPUs may work better
for certain cases.

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:39.160
TPUs might work better
for other cases.

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:42.400
But the power of Kubeflow
is I was able to experiment

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:45.040
with those various
deployments, without making

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:48.430
any significant code changes--
just changing some parameters

00:21:48.430 --> 00:21:51.800
and recompiling and I
was able to get that.

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:53.710
So now we're going to
do one final thing.

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:57.760
Remember our goal here was to
provide sentiment analysis.

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:00.850
You can see there we deployed
a brand new server to take

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:03.310
advantage of that model
that we have now trained,

00:22:03.310 --> 00:22:05.260
and we're going to go
back to the Yelp text

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:07.150
and see how we did.

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:11.690
You can see-- remember, happy,
sad-- happy was not accurate.

00:22:11.690 --> 00:22:14.080
Let's see what happens
when we swap it out,

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:16.570
and we have the predict,
and it works fine.

00:22:16.570 --> 00:22:19.870
And so now, again, in a
very straightforward way,

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:22.450
using very standard models
that are published out there,

00:22:22.450 --> 00:22:27.130
we were able to train on our
data set this Yelp reviews

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:36.200
and see a result that improved
our overall application.

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:40.840
So as you saw, very clean,
very straightforward way

00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:44.920
to deploy machine learning to a
variety of different locations,

00:22:44.920 --> 00:22:47.080
based on your business
needs and based

00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:50.360
on the hardware and software
that you have available to you.

00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:52.320
What you didn't see
was anything bespoke.

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:55.810
I was using native,
open source technologies

00:22:55.810 --> 00:22:58.990
to solve these problems, using
standard machine learning

00:22:58.990 --> 00:23:00.760
models I was able to deploy.

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:04.330
And what that means is greatly
reduces the amount of time

00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:06.700
your software engineers,
your data pros, your data

00:23:06.700 --> 00:23:11.530
scientists, your IT pros have to
spend customizing and building

00:23:11.530 --> 00:23:13.130
their solutions together.

00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:16.030
You also didn't see any
cloud-specific, non-portable

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:16.870
technology.

00:23:16.870 --> 00:23:20.200
The exact same deployment I
had locally ran on the cloud,

00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:22.660
and all I had to do was
change the parameters.

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:25.847
And finally, you didn't see any
forking of the Kubernetes APIs.

00:23:25.847 --> 00:23:28.180
This isn't something where
you need to go to your IT pro

00:23:28.180 --> 00:23:31.570
and say they have to take all
the software that they spent

00:23:31.570 --> 00:23:34.570
rolling out, make it very easy
to deploy their applications

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:37.290
using Kubernetes, and
change it under the hood

00:23:37.290 --> 00:23:40.490
to now support your brand new
machine learning workloads.

00:23:40.490 --> 00:23:43.900
This uses native
Kubernetes APIs.

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:47.830
And really, we are at the start
of a brand new world here.

00:23:47.830 --> 00:23:50.220
We want to use
data to save lives.

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:52.570
There's this wonderful
story out of Sweden

00:23:52.570 --> 00:23:55.390
where a bunch of data scientists
looked at their hospital

00:23:55.390 --> 00:23:57.940
records, and they
discovered that women

00:23:57.940 --> 00:23:59.800
and stay-at-home
parents were being

00:23:59.800 --> 00:24:03.250
injured at a disproportionate
rate after new snowfalls.

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:05.530
By looking at what
happened, they

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:09.250
realized that by doing nothing
more than changing the plowing

00:24:09.250 --> 00:24:11.910
schedule for those
particular situations,

00:24:11.910 --> 00:24:15.610
after new snowfalls, they
were able to plow sidewalks

00:24:15.610 --> 00:24:16.900
before roads.

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:18.640
And what that
meant was that they

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:21.250
were able to save lives
and reduce injuries.

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:24.340
And that's a wonderful
story because that's really

00:24:24.340 --> 00:24:28.270
what we're trying to unlock with
machine learning and Kubeflow.

00:24:28.270 --> 00:24:32.070
We want to give people who are
not experts in data science

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:34.420
the intelligence to
make the difference

00:24:34.420 --> 00:24:36.310
for their overall solutions.

00:24:36.310 --> 00:24:41.710
And for us, that means
we want to give nurses

00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:43.420
and civil engineers
and professors

00:24:43.420 --> 00:24:46.165
all the information necessary
for them to understand

00:24:46.165 --> 00:24:50.350
what is affecting their
world and how to make better

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:54.020
decisions using this data.

00:24:54.020 --> 00:24:57.380
So finally, we are
just getting started.

00:24:57.380 --> 00:24:59.810
You can see here there are
a number of different folks

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:03.320
who have contributed to
the Kubeflow platform.

00:25:03.320 --> 00:25:05.520
We announced
Kubeflow in December

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:07.670
at KubeCon in North America.

00:25:07.670 --> 00:25:10.550
And over the past six
months, we have been stunned

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:14.120
with how quickly it has grown.

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:18.530
Over 700 commits, 71
different contributors,

00:25:18.530 --> 00:25:21.530
17 different companies-- you
can see some of them here--

00:25:21.530 --> 00:25:23.690
who are already
contributing the platform.

00:25:23.690 --> 00:25:26.630
And we really do want to make
this an open platform that

00:25:26.630 --> 00:25:28.670
is designed to run everywhere.

00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:32.060
As you saw, we went from laptop
to Google Container Engine.

00:25:32.060 --> 00:25:36.170
We support anywhere you are
running a Kubernetes compliant

00:25:36.170 --> 00:25:37.220
architecture.

00:25:37.220 --> 00:25:40.820
On-prem, in the
cloud, on your laptop,

00:25:40.820 --> 00:25:42.910
we support Kubernetes
compliance.

00:25:42.910 --> 00:25:47.390
For what's next, we plan on
getting to our 0.2 release.

00:25:47.390 --> 00:25:49.370
That's going to
happen this summer,

00:25:49.370 --> 00:25:54.625
and we have some focus around
getting to much easier setup.

00:25:54.625 --> 00:25:56.750
Right now, unfortunately,
does require a little bit

00:25:56.750 --> 00:26:00.800
of understanding of ksonnet
and native Kubeflow components.

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:03.710
We want to make that very, very
easy for you to start with.

00:26:03.710 --> 00:26:07.940
Second, we really want to
focus on taking advantage

00:26:07.940 --> 00:26:10.280
of all the work that the
Kubernetes community is

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:11.240
currently doing.

00:26:11.240 --> 00:26:13.077
We want to abstract
away a number

00:26:13.077 --> 00:26:14.660
of the different
components, including

00:26:14.660 --> 00:26:17.224
auto-scaling,
improved accelerators.

00:26:17.224 --> 00:26:19.640
We want to make it so that
your data scientists can simply

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:23.600
describe declaratively what they
would like to have this job run

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:27.840
as, and Kubernetes takes care
of all the stuff under the hood.

00:26:27.840 --> 00:26:30.800
We also want to support more
packages and frameworks.

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:33.094
While you saw a demonstration
with TensorFlow,

00:26:33.094 --> 00:26:35.510
we have a number of folks in
the community who are already

00:26:35.510 --> 00:26:39.380
contributing, PyTorch, Caffe.

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:41.810
We've heard requests for
[INAUDIBLE] and MXNet

00:26:41.810 --> 00:26:44.210
and so on and so forth, and
we want to support that as

00:26:44.210 --> 00:26:45.800
much as possible.

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:48.410
And in particular, if there
are particular scenarios

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:52.430
that you're hearing from or
you'd like us to extend to,

00:26:52.430 --> 00:26:54.800
we'd love to hear that next.

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:58.790
And then finally, the
best feature that we like

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:00.770
is the one that we
haven't heard yet.

00:27:00.770 --> 00:27:03.210
It is a completely
open platform.

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:04.430
We have an open community.

00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:08.300
Everything is done in the public
via our open source repository,

00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:10.320
and it really is an open design.

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:13.190
One of the things that we
learn best about Kubernetes

00:27:13.190 --> 00:27:15.440
was really listening
to all the ways

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:18.170
that people wanted to use the
platform that it didn't yet

00:27:18.170 --> 00:27:18.950
support.

00:27:18.950 --> 00:27:20.780
That helped us
understand the ways

00:27:20.780 --> 00:27:23.810
that we should extend, the
places we should invest in,

00:27:23.810 --> 00:27:28.820
and how to do new open ideas
and open source development.

00:27:28.820 --> 00:27:31.380
And we really are open.

00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:32.930
We have an open source repo.

00:27:32.930 --> 00:27:34.250
You can come in any time.

00:27:34.250 --> 00:27:35.450
You can contribute.

00:27:35.450 --> 00:27:36.680
We have Slack channels.

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:38.360
We have email lists.

00:27:38.360 --> 00:27:41.300
We have GitHub
with issues, and we

00:27:41.300 --> 00:27:44.120
want to hear more than
anything what to do next.

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:46.850
And while it is a 0.1 release
and maybe a little bit

00:27:46.850 --> 00:27:49.550
early for your environment,
all the components

00:27:49.550 --> 00:27:51.910
that are running inside
it are battle tested.

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:54.710
There are TensorFlow and
JupyterHub and Ambassador

00:27:54.710 --> 00:27:57.890
and [? Selden ?] and
TensorFlow serving.

00:27:57.890 --> 00:27:59.990
These are things that
have been out in the wild

00:27:59.990 --> 00:28:02.270
for a long, long
time, and they are

00:28:02.270 --> 00:28:05.810
more than ready to experiment
with and give us feedback.

00:28:05.810 --> 00:28:09.620
And so with that,
I'm going to wrap up.

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:11.530
Please stay tuned
for the live Q&amp;A.

00:30:30.710 --> 00:30:33.350
Thank you very much,
and welcome back.

00:30:33.350 --> 00:30:35.370
So we have a lot of questions.

00:30:35.370 --> 00:30:37.730
We'll try get through them all.

00:30:37.730 --> 00:30:40.070
The first one, do you
support other frameworks

00:30:40.070 --> 00:30:41.630
besides TensorFlow?

00:30:41.630 --> 00:30:43.580
Yes, absolutely.

00:30:43.580 --> 00:30:45.320
We started with
TensorFlow because,

00:30:45.320 --> 00:30:47.330
at Google, that's what
we're familiar with,

00:30:47.330 --> 00:30:50.750
and that's what the teams
already had ready to go.

00:30:50.750 --> 00:30:53.780
We absolutely want to
support other frameworks.

00:30:53.780 --> 00:30:55.370
We want to support
every framework.

00:30:55.370 --> 00:30:58.610
We want to have them as native
packages that we test nightly

00:30:58.610 --> 00:30:59.710
and roll them out.

00:30:59.710 --> 00:31:01.910
And so more than
anything, we are really

00:31:01.910 --> 00:31:04.010
looking for the community
to help us prioritize.

00:31:04.010 --> 00:31:06.950
Give us your feedback and
we will help bubble things

00:31:06.950 --> 00:31:07.910
to the top.

00:31:07.910 --> 00:31:11.327
As I mentioned, we do have
several operators already

00:31:11.327 --> 00:31:13.160
checked into the codebase
that you can start

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:15.080
experimenting with right now.

00:31:15.080 --> 00:31:18.230
But it is absolutely
a core of Kubeflow

00:31:18.230 --> 00:31:21.260
to support all the machine
learning frameworks

00:31:21.260 --> 00:31:23.180
that people would want.

00:31:23.180 --> 00:31:26.000
Can I run Kubeflow
outside of Google Cloud?

00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:26.900
Absolutely.

00:31:26.900 --> 00:31:30.650
As you saw, we support all
the way from your laptop

00:31:30.650 --> 00:31:35.190
to any Kubernetes compliant
cloud or a deployment.

00:31:35.190 --> 00:31:39.320
So whether or not that you're
on-prem, hybrid, Google Cloud,

00:31:39.320 --> 00:31:42.200
another cloud, as long as
it's Kubernetes compliant,

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:43.116
it should run great.

00:31:43.116 --> 00:31:44.990
If not, we want to hear
about it because that

00:31:44.990 --> 00:31:46.950
is part of our core mission.

00:31:46.950 --> 00:31:48.680
How do I customize Kubeflow?

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:51.800
So as you saw there are
some standard packages.

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:55.370
Kubeflow core and
others that run inside

00:31:55.370 --> 00:31:57.020
in our overall registry.

00:31:57.020 --> 00:32:00.290
We actually support a number of
different ways to configure it.

00:32:00.290 --> 00:32:03.290
Kubeflow uses
ksonnet from Heptio

00:32:03.290 --> 00:32:07.040
to create packages that are
highly customizable that use

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:08.720
parameters and other things.

00:32:08.720 --> 00:32:11.390
And you can either
download and tweak

00:32:11.390 --> 00:32:14.120
those using parameters or
the native extension points,

00:32:14.120 --> 00:32:15.890
or you can write your own.

00:32:15.890 --> 00:32:19.220
And you don't have to submit
those to a central registry.

00:32:19.220 --> 00:32:22.610
You can have your own private
registry that Kubeflow can use

00:32:22.610 --> 00:32:26.330
or any of your team can use
that's appropriate for you.

00:32:26.330 --> 00:32:28.730
What's the roadmap for Kubeflow?

00:32:28.730 --> 00:32:32.300
We expect, as I mentioned,
to hit 0.2 to this summer.

00:32:32.300 --> 00:32:35.600
And shortly thereafter,
we expect to go straight

00:32:35.600 --> 00:32:41.330
to 1.0 in our GA release.

00:32:41.330 --> 00:32:43.610
More than anything,
we want to accelerate

00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:46.020
peoples' solutions with ML.

00:32:46.020 --> 00:32:47.960
And so for us, as
soon as we feel

00:32:47.960 --> 00:32:51.170
like we can have that production
ready environment that actually

00:32:51.170 --> 00:32:55.340
solves solutions today, we're
very excited to hit 1.0.

00:32:55.340 --> 00:32:58.260
More than anything, we are
ready to listen to you.

00:32:58.260 --> 00:33:01.866
And so if there are
key features missing

00:33:01.866 --> 00:33:03.240
we would love to
hear about them.

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:05.390
If it feels like all
the features are there

00:33:05.390 --> 00:33:08.210
and it's just a matter of
finishing off and getting

00:33:08.210 --> 00:33:10.490
everything production ready,
we can focus on that too.

00:33:10.490 --> 00:33:13.520
So more than anything,
we'd love to hear from you.

00:33:13.520 --> 00:33:16.580
Do you support ML
accelerators in Kubeflow?

00:33:16.580 --> 00:33:23.510
Absolutely, the machine learning
framework for Kubernetes

00:33:23.510 --> 00:33:27.950
has done a bunch of work,
relative to ML and ML

00:33:27.950 --> 00:33:29.330
accelerators.

00:33:29.330 --> 00:33:32.600
This has been with contributions
from Google, from NVIDIA,

00:33:32.600 --> 00:33:34.130
from Intel--

00:33:34.130 --> 00:33:36.590
a number of different
folks making sure

00:33:36.590 --> 00:33:39.440
that the way to
extend at Kubernetes

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:40.800
is very straightforward.

00:33:40.800 --> 00:33:43.130
So you can pick and
choose the accelerators

00:33:43.130 --> 00:33:47.210
that are native to you, without
having to do customization,

00:33:47.210 --> 00:33:48.650
without having
necessarily to run

00:33:48.650 --> 00:33:50.900
a different version of
your container runtime

00:33:50.900 --> 00:33:54.120
in order to support
ML or accelerators.

00:33:54.120 --> 00:33:57.930
Kubeflow, our goal is to
not do any of that stuff.

00:33:57.930 --> 00:34:00.620
We want to leverage all the
work that the Kubernetes team is

00:34:00.620 --> 00:34:03.350
doing under the
hood and simply make

00:34:03.350 --> 00:34:07.310
it available via annotations
or higher level constructs

00:34:07.310 --> 00:34:09.949
that you can describe
your ML workloads

00:34:09.949 --> 00:34:12.050
and say I'd like
to roll this out.

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:14.510
And I'd like to use this
type of accelerator here,

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:17.090
or I'd like to use that
type of accelerator there.

00:34:17.090 --> 00:34:21.110
And Kubernetes will take care of
the hard stuff under the hood.

00:34:21.110 --> 00:34:24.920
Are there any detailed tutorials
on which you can deep dive?

00:34:24.920 --> 00:34:30.409
Absolutely, we have workshops,
tutorials on GitHub.

00:34:30.409 --> 00:34:34.790
I highly recommend a number
of walkthroughs and videos

00:34:34.790 --> 00:34:36.020
that people have done.

00:34:36.020 --> 00:34:39.019
I'd also suggest going to
our examples directory.

00:34:39.019 --> 00:34:41.060
We already have a number
of different really cool

00:34:41.060 --> 00:34:42.090
examples--

00:34:42.090 --> 00:34:45.050
one we did in partnership
with the team from GitHub

00:34:45.050 --> 00:34:47.630
around doing GitHub issue
summarization to make

00:34:47.630 --> 00:34:49.340
it very easy to
understand what's

00:34:49.340 --> 00:34:52.040
happening in larger
GitHub repos.

00:34:52.040 --> 00:34:54.210
That's all built
on top of Kubeflow.

00:34:54.210 --> 00:34:56.719
And as you saw
there, it is our goal

00:34:56.719 --> 00:34:59.360
to make available a
number of different models

00:34:59.360 --> 00:35:03.470
just like you saw with
Tensor2Tensor to make

00:35:03.470 --> 00:35:07.010
it very straightforward to
download standard models

00:35:07.010 --> 00:35:08.330
and run them.

00:35:08.330 --> 00:35:10.400
Part of what we're trying
to do with Kubeflow

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:13.950
really is solve the
reproducibility crisis.

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:17.959
In machine learning, it
is a very common scenario

00:35:17.959 --> 00:35:20.000
where you might have a
machine learning scientist

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:23.510
at one organization who
publishes their research,

00:35:23.510 --> 00:35:27.770
and it basically is impossible
for any other data scientist

00:35:27.770 --> 00:35:30.300
to reproduce that experiment.

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:33.000
Our goal, our long-term
vision with Kubeflow

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:35.700
is really solve that
and have everyone

00:35:35.700 --> 00:35:40.320
be able to publish your
Kubeflow code, your model,

00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:44.100
and your data, and make
that an entire package that

00:35:44.100 --> 00:35:48.630
allows anyone to reproduce
a given machine learning

00:35:48.630 --> 00:35:50.520
solution.

00:35:50.520 --> 00:35:53.440
And so you'll see that over
and over and over again.

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:56.340
We are out there working
with researchers and machine

00:35:56.340 --> 00:35:58.110
learning data
scientists to figure out

00:35:58.110 --> 00:36:00.090
how to make that happen.

00:36:00.090 --> 00:36:03.780
Finally, when would I use
Kubeflow over Cloud Machine

00:36:03.780 --> 00:36:05.490
Learning Engine?

00:36:05.490 --> 00:36:08.010
Cloud Machine Learning Engine
is an offering from Google

00:36:08.010 --> 00:36:11.880
that offers hosted
prediction and inference,

00:36:11.880 --> 00:36:14.280
and the fact is that's
exactly what Kubeflow

00:36:14.280 --> 00:36:15.630
is designed to do.

00:36:15.630 --> 00:36:17.980
Machine Learning Engine
is a hosted solution.

00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:22.230
So if you don't want to think
about scaling up or using GPUs

00:36:22.230 --> 00:36:26.100
or not using GPUs, worry
about serving or anything

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:29.490
like that, it offers a
fantastic endpoint for you

00:36:29.490 --> 00:36:33.600
to upload your model and
Cloud Machine Learning Engine

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:35.060
takes care of the rest.

00:36:35.060 --> 00:36:37.140
Kubeflow is highly composable.

00:36:37.140 --> 00:36:40.860
We offer an orchestration
engine built in called Argo,

00:36:40.860 --> 00:36:43.630
from the Argo Project,
which is also open source.

00:36:43.630 --> 00:36:46.050
And from that, it offers a
number of different steps

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:47.970
to walk through
all the components

00:36:47.970 --> 00:36:51.810
that you saw on some of
those earlier slides.

00:36:51.810 --> 00:36:55.090
In any one of those steps,
you could say, actually,

00:36:55.090 --> 00:36:57.330
I don't want to use
the component that

00:36:57.330 --> 00:36:58.860
was built into Kubeflow.

00:36:58.860 --> 00:37:03.210
I'd really prefer to use this
hosted software as a service,

00:37:03.210 --> 00:37:04.860
just handle this
particular step,

00:37:04.860 --> 00:37:06.690
whether or not that's
training or inference

00:37:06.690 --> 00:37:08.320
or anything along the lines.

00:37:08.320 --> 00:37:10.780
You can instruct
Kubeflow to say, OK,

00:37:10.780 --> 00:37:12.580
you've reached this
step, now call out

00:37:12.580 --> 00:37:13.980
to this external service.

00:37:13.980 --> 00:37:16.440
Once you're done, call
back to this webhook

00:37:16.440 --> 00:37:18.060
and I'm ready to go.

00:37:18.060 --> 00:37:19.440
And that really is it.

00:37:19.440 --> 00:37:21.370
The idea is that
Kubeflow provides

00:37:21.370 --> 00:37:24.030
this more general
orchestration, framework,

00:37:24.030 --> 00:37:26.550
and stack that you
can swap in and out.

00:37:26.550 --> 00:37:31.320
If you want to customize or
self-host or do something

00:37:31.320 --> 00:37:33.600
for yourself, then use Kubeflow.

00:37:33.600 --> 00:37:35.820
If you'd like there to be
a managed solution that

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:37.571
took care of everything
for you, then you

00:37:37.571 --> 00:37:39.570
might use something like
Machine Learning Engine

00:37:39.570 --> 00:37:41.370
for that particular step.

00:37:41.370 --> 00:37:45.510
And I should reiterate that that
is a key component of Kubeflow.

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:49.380
We want to make it very,
very easy for you to deploy

00:37:49.380 --> 00:37:52.590
and plug-in to your existing
legacy applications.

00:37:52.590 --> 00:37:53.970
And so whether or
not you already

00:37:53.970 --> 00:37:58.860
have a data processing solution,
which is extremely common.

00:37:58.860 --> 00:38:02.100
Arguably, anyone who's doing
a certain amount of data

00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:04.560
processing today
or are collecting

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:06.060
a certain amount
of data today has

00:38:06.060 --> 00:38:07.950
some data processing pipelines.

00:38:07.950 --> 00:38:09.090
That's fantastic.

00:38:09.090 --> 00:38:11.700
As long as you're
able to surface

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:14.550
the processing of that data
to Kubeflow, that's great.

00:38:14.550 --> 00:38:16.620
You don't have to run
that particular component

00:38:16.620 --> 00:38:17.760
inside Kubeflow.

00:38:17.760 --> 00:38:20.460
You can simply connect
the component to Kubeflow

00:38:20.460 --> 00:38:22.170
to that data
processing pipeline,

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:24.620
and it should run fine.

00:38:24.620 --> 00:38:27.740
And so with that,
we're wrapped up

00:38:27.740 --> 00:38:29.420
with all our Kubeflow questions.

00:38:29.420 --> 00:38:33.830
We'll be online and available
to answer any further questions.

00:38:33.830 --> 00:38:35.900
Or as you saw with
all of the community

00:38:35.900 --> 00:38:38.540
framework and connection
points, you're

00:38:38.540 --> 00:38:40.340
more than welcome
to come and jump

00:38:40.340 --> 00:38:42.440
in and ask us
questions directly,

00:38:42.440 --> 00:38:45.470
and we try to be
very, very responsive.

00:38:45.470 --> 00:38:47.630
With that, stay tuned
for the next session--

00:38:47.630 --> 00:38:51.500
Cloud Armor, Protecting
Your Application From

00:38:51.500 --> 00:38:53.900
DDOS Attacks With Google.

00:38:53.900 --> 00:38:57.470
And thank you very
much for the time.

00:38:57.470 --> 00:39:00.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

